## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of deduplication in real systems, combining transactional patterns, Kafka exactly-once semantics, and robust auditing.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once processing
- Transactional outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions (transactional.id)
- Idempotency keys and dedupe stores
- Compensating actions and audit trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: within a DB transaction
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET status='PAID' WHERE id = :orderId;
INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event_type, payload, created_at) VALUES (:orderId, 'OrderPaid', :payload, NOW());
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Kafka publish path (conceptual)
producer.beginTransaction();
producer.send(outboxEvent);
producer.commitTransaction();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate duplicates under failure modes (partition revocation, broker failure)?
- What metrics and logs would you instrument to verify end-to-end exactly-once behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant budgets, dynamic throttling, and robust rollback strategies in a multi-tenant, latency-sensitive service.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Burn rate calculation and budget ledger
- Edge throttling (token bucket) and graceful degradation
- Canary rollouts and automated rollback
- Observability with per-tenant metrics and PromQL

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: update per-tenant budget on each request
function onRequest(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['tenant-id'];
  const now = Date.now();
  const budget = ledger.getBudget(tenant);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) throttle(req, tenant);
  else { forward(req); ledger.consume(tenant, estimateCost(req)); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this system under simulated traffic bursts?
- What thresholds would you choose for alerting and auto-rollback cadence?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesss real-world ability to design resilient, low-tail-latency multi-region pipelines under stress while preserving tenant fairness and clear rollback paths.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and circuit breaking in streaming pipelines
- Per-tenant QoS and traffic shaping
- Exactly-once processing guarantees across Kafka and sinks
- Observability for SLO-driven tail latency
- Chaos testing and incident rollback discipline

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bounded per-tenant backpressure
function handleEvent(event, tenant) {
  const q = queues[tenant];
  if (q.length < MAX_PER_TENANT) {
    enqueue(event, tenant);
  } else {
    drop(event); // apply backpressure upstream
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the degradation plan with synthetic workloads?
- What metrics would you use to decide when to restore normal traffic?
